+`forum://{pub}` using locations it was given and the `known_copies` of verified heads it holds. Locations remain
+untrusted hints; only the signed bytes establish authority.
 
 The reference implementation continues after a valid response to seek a newer fully verified head. It tries each
-normalized location once per fetch, including new hints from fully verified publications. Each accepted head is checked
-against the head then held (§8), so later stale or conflicting responses cannot replace it. Failures from individual
+normalized location once per fetch, including its last successful HTTP source and new hints from fully verified publications.
+It queues explicit locations first, then the stored source, then signed hints; up to four locations run concurrently and
+results are processed as they finish. Discovery is limited to 64 locations per fetch, with a warning when more are
+available. Each location has a 30-second deadline spanning its head and missing revisions/assets. Cancellation stops
+new work and waits for started workers to finish. These are reference-implementation limits, not wire-format rules.
+
+Each accepted head is checked against the head then held (§8) again inside the database write transaction, so concurrent
+fetches, imports and local publishing cannot replace it with a stale or conflicting head. For distinct signed heads at
+the same sequence, the first committed head remains held and later conflicting heads are rejected; this is not consensus.
+Failures from individual
 locations are retained alongside the best successful result. This discovers the newest fully verified copy reached,
 not a guarantee of global freshness when other copies are unknown or unreachable.
 
